         <div type="bio">
            <p>German and French poet. Born in Alsace-Lorraine, he met JJ during WWI in Zurich. He became a representative of the Rhein-Verlag in Paris and proposed a French translation of <name type="work" key="W_APORTRAIT">A Portrait of the Artist as a Young Man</name>, which failed to materialize. He participated in the translation of “Anna Livie Plurabelle” (<name type="work" key="ID_MISSINg">La Nouvelle revue française</name>, 1931) and published a French translation of “Ecce Puer” in 1933 along with several articles on JJ. As a poet he was loosely affiliated with Surrealism, collaborated with his wife <name type="person" key="goll90">Claire Goll</name> (1891-1977) and founded the magazine <name type="work" key="ID_MISSINg">Surréalisme</name> in October 1924. He spent the WWII years in New York and died in Paris. <hi rend="bold">William Brockman</hi>
                </p>
         </div>
